---
// The terms. Two different things are covered and they are kept apart on purpose: the software,
// which is AGPL and carries the licence's terms, and the services we run, which are ours.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const UPDATED = "22 September 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Terms · Surfing Dog"
  description="The licence on the software, and the terms for the services we run: this website, the docs, the requests board and the directory."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap-read section is-head" id="terms" aria-labelledby="terms-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Terms</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="terms-h">The software, and the services</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The software is open source and yours to run. The services we run, like this website, have the terms below.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">Last updated {UPDATED}. Surfing Dog Lda, Portugal. Questions: hello@surfingdog.ai</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="sw-h">
    <div class="wrap wrap-read prose">
      <h2 class="h-section" id="sw-h">The software</h2>
      <p>
        Surfing Dog Inbox is licensed under the <b>GNU Affero General Public License v3.0</b>. You may run it, read
        it, change it and share it. If you run a changed version as a service for other people, the licence asks you to
        offer them your changes. The spec and the SDK are MIT instead, and the connector SDK will be too when
        it ships, so that talking to an inbox puts no copyleft obligation on anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        The licence text in the repository is what applies. Like other open-source licences, it says the software comes
        with no warranty and the authors are not liable for what happens when you run it. If you run it, you are the
        one running it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">The services we run</h2>
      <p>
        This website, the documentation, the requests board and the directory are services we run. By using them you
        accept the terms below. If you don't accept them, please don't use them. The software works without any of them.
      </p>

      <h3 class="h-card">What you may not do</h3>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>Post anything unlawful, or anything you have no right to post.</li>
        <li>Pretend to be someone else, or claim to speak for a business that isn't yours.</li>
        <li>Attack the services, or try to reach data that isn't yours.</li>
        <li>Scrape so heavily that the service gets worse for other people.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We may remove a post or refuse service for any of these reasons. If we do, and you ask, we'll tell you which
        one.
      </p>

      <h3 class="h-card">The requests board</h3>
      <p>
        What you post there is public and stays public. You still own what you write, and you give us permission to
        show it on the site. Votes decide what we look at first. They don't promise that anything gets built, and
        nothing said on the board is a commitment to ship.
      </p>

      <h3 class="h-card">Availability</h3>
      <p>
        These services are free and offered as they are, with no uptime guarantee and no warranty. We may change or
        withdraw them. If something goes down, we fix it because we use it too. No contract obliges us to.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Hosting, when it opens</h2>
      <p>
        Paid hosting is not open yet. When it opens, it will have its own agreement covering what a paid service needs
        and this page leaves out: what the service promises, what it costs, how to leave and take your data with you,
        and a written data processing agreement. Nothing on this page is an offer to sell anything.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Liability</h2>
      <p>
        As far as the law allows, we are not liable for indirect or consequential loss, lost profit or lost data
        arising from the free services or the software. Nothing here limits liability that the law does not let us
        limit, such as for death or personal injury caused by negligence, or for fraud. If you are a consumer, your
        statutory rights are unaffected.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Law</h2>
      <p>
        Portuguese law and the courts of Portugal apply. If you are a consumer in the European Union, you keep the
        protection of the law where you live and can bring a claim there.
      </p>

      <div class="glass card" style="margin-top: var(--sp-6)">
        <h3 class="h-card">Where this stands</h3>
        <p class="card-text">
          We wrote this ourselves, and a lawyer has not reviewed it yet. That review will happen before we sell
          anything, and this note comes off the page once it has.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
